          <compound>
            <id type="integer">891</id>
            <name>FMNH2</name>
            <hmdb-id>HMDB0001142</hmdb-id>
            <description>FMNH2 is the reduced form of flavin mononucleotide. It is a substrate of the enzyme FMN reductase (EC 1.5.1.29), an enzyme that catalyzes the chemical reaction FMNH2 + NAD(P)+ &lt;=&gt; FMN + NAD(P)H + H+. Flavin mononucleotide (FMN), or riboflavin-5&amp;prime;-phosphate, is a biomolecule produced from riboflavin (vitamin B2) by the enzyme riboflavin kinase and functions as prosthetic group of various oxidoreductases including NADH dehydrogenase. During a catalytic cycle, the reversible interconversion of oxidized (FMN), semiquinone (FMNH&amp;bull;) and reduced (FMNH2) forms occurs in the various oxidoreductases. FMN is a stronger oxidizing agent than NAD and is particularly useful because it can take part in both one- and two-electron transfers.</description>
